1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,560
But the two in the hut, Odysseus and the noble swineherd,

2
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:16,220
had lit a fire and were readying breakfast at dawn,
and had sent the herdsmen out with the gathered swine;

3
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:22,860
and around Telemachus the yapping dogs went fawning,
and did not bark as he came. And noble Odysseus noticed

4
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,340
the dogs fawning, and the sound of footsteps came to him.

5
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:30,040
At once he spoke winged words to Eumaeus:

6
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:33,220
"Eumaeus, surely some companion of yours is coming,

7
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:39,700
or another man well known, since the dogs are not barking
but fawn around him; and I hear the thud of feet."

8
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:45,980
The word was not all spoken when his own dear son
stood in the gateway. The swineherd sprang up amazed,

9
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,860
and out of his hands fell the vessels he was busy with,

10
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:54,600
mixing the sparkling wine. He went to meet his master,
and kissed his head, and both his beautiful eyes,

11
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,100
and both his hands; and a warm tear fell from him.

12
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:03,820
And as a father, in the fullness of his heart,
embraces his son come from a far country in the tenth year,

13
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:08,040
an only son, late-born,
for whom he has suffered many sorrows —

14
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,740
so then the noble swineherd took godlike Telemachus

15
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:17,420
in his arms and kissed him all over, as one escaped
from death; and sobbing he spoke winged words to him:

16
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:20,660
"You have come, Telemachus — sweet light! I told myself

17
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:24,220
I would never see you again,
once you had gone by ship to Pylos.

18
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:30,660
Come in now, come, dear child, so my heart may take
its joy of looking at you, newly here from elsewhere.

19
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,280
You do not often visit the farm and the herdsmen;

20
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:39,080
you stay in town: so, it seems, it has pleased your heart —
to look on that deadly crowd of the suitor-men."

21
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:41,500
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

22
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,080
"So be it, old father.
It is for your sake I have come here,

23
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:50,540
to see you with my eyes, and to hear you tell me
whether my mother still remains in the halls, or by now

24
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:57,420
some other man has married her, and Odysseus' bed lies
empty of sleepers, holding only foul spider-webs."

25
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,560
Then the swineherd answered him, a leader of men:

26
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:05,140
"Indeed she remains, with an enduring heart,
in your own halls; and always in misery

27
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:07,980
her nights and her days waste away in weeping."

28
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:13,380
So speaking, he took the bronze spear from him,
and Telemachus went in and crossed the stone threshold.

29
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:20,060
As he came on, his father Odysseus yielded his seat;
but Telemachus, from his side, checked him and said:

30
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:25,360
"Sit, stranger. We will find a seat elsewhere in our
own farmstead. Here is the man who will set one."

31
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:29,440
So he spoke; and Odysseus went back
and sat down. And the swineherd

32
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:35,540
strewed green brushwood under, and a fleece on top,
and there the dear son of Odysseus seated himself.

33
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:38,000
And the swineherd set before them platters of roasted

34
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,700
meat, which they had left from their eating the day
before, and briskly heaped up bread in the baskets,

35
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:49,600
and mixed honey-sweet wine in an ivy-wood bowl,
and himself sat down opposite godlike Odysseus.

36
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,340
And their hands went out to the
good things spread before them.

37
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:58,460
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,
then Telemachus spoke to the noble swineherd:

38
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:02,340
"Old father, where has this guest
of yours come from? How did sailors

39
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:05,240
bring him to Ithaca? Who did they claim to be?

40
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:07,400
For I hardly think he came here on foot."

41
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:09,880
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

42
00:03:09,880 --> 00:03:12,620
"Then I will tell you, child, the whole truth of it.

43
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:16,340
He claims his birth from Crete
of the wide dancing-grounds,

44
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:21,460
and says he has wandered rolling through many
cities of mortal men: so a god has spun it for him.

45
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:27,380
Now, run off from a ship of Thesprotian men,
he has come to my farmstead; and I put him in your hands.

46
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:30,780
Do as you will. He declares himself your suppliant."

47
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:33,260
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

48
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:36,100
"Eumaeus, this word of yours cuts to the heart.

49
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:38,320
How am I to receive this guest in my house?

50
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:46,000
I am young myself, and have no faith yet in my hands
to defend a man, when someone picks a quarrel first.

51
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,060
And my mother's heart is divided in her breast, two ways:

52
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:56,340
whether to stay here with me and keep the house,
respecting her husband's bed and the voice of the people,

53
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:02,400
or to follow now whichever of the Achaeans is best
among her suitors in the halls, and offers the most.

54
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:05,780
But as for this guest, since he has come to your house,

55
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:09,540
I will dress him in a cloak and a tunic, fine clothing,

56
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:13,340
and give him a two-edged sword, and sandals for his feet,

57
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:16,400
and send him wherever his heart and spirit bid him go.

58
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:22,580
Or if you will, keep him and care for him at the farmstead,
and I will send clothing here, and all his bread

59
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:26,780
to eat, so he will not wear you down, you and your men.

60
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:32,040
But in among the suitors I would not let him go:
too reckless is the insolence they carry —

61
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:35,520
they would mock him,
and it would be a terrible grief to me.

62
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:42,620
It is hard for one man to get anything done among many,
even a strong man: they are far the more powerful."

63
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:45,260
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus spoke to him:

64
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:51,280
"Friend — since surely it is right for me to answer
also — truly my heart is eaten through as I listen,

65
00:04:51,280 --> 00:04:53,380
hearing you tell what reckless things the suitors

66
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,500
devise in the halls, against
the will of such a man as you.

67
00:04:57,500 --> 00:05:00,340
Tell me: do you bow to it willingly, or do the people

68
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:03,700
through the land hate you, following the voice of a god?

69
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:06,600
Or have you some fault to find with brothers — those a man

70
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:10,260
trusts to fight beside him, though a great feud rise?

71
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:12,780
Ah, would that I were as young as this anger of mine,

72
00:05:12,780 --> 00:05:16,500
or were a son of blameless Odysseus, or the man himself —

73
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:19,420
then straightway might some
stranger cut the head from my neck

74
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:25,460
if I did not walk into the hall of Laertes' son
Odysseus, and become an evil to every man of them.

75
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:31,120
And if they overpowered me by their numbers,
alone as I am, I would rather die cut down in my own halls

76
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:34,180
than look on forever at these disgraceful doings:

77
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:39,400
guests roughly handled, and men hauling the
slave-women shamefully through the beautiful house,

78
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:42,700
and wine drawn off and squandered,
and them eating my bread

79
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:47,580
idly, recklessly, endlessly —
for a work never to be finished."

80
00:05:47,580 --> 00:05:49,960
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

81
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:53,500
"Then I will tell you, stranger, exactly how it is.

82
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,760
It is not that the whole people
hates me and bears a grudge,

83
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:03,060
nor have I fault to find with brothers — those a man
trusts to fight beside him, though a great feud rise.

84
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:06,740
For so the son of Cronos has
made ours a line of only sons:

85
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:12,680
Arceisius fathered Laertes, an only son;
his father got Odysseus, an only son; and Odysseus

86
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:17,320
fathered only me, and left me in
the halls, and had no joy of me.

87
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:20,000
And that is why enemies past counting are in the house.

88
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,120
All the great men who hold power among the islands —
Dulichium and Same and wooded Zacynthus —

89
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:27,500
and all who lord it through rocky Ithaca:

90
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:30,400
so many woo my mother, and wear this house away.

91
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:37,100
And she neither refuses the marriage she loathes,
nor can she bring it to an end. And they eat on, wasting

92
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:40,720
my house. Soon they will tear me to pieces too.

93
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:44,080
But surely these things lie on the knees of the gods.

94
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:47,680
Old father, you go — quickly —
and tell steady-hearted Penelope

95
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:51,360
that I am safe for her, and have come back from Pylos.

96
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:54,160
I will stay here. And you return to this place

97
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:57,980
when you have told her — her only.
Let none of the other Achaeans

98
00:06:57,980 --> 00:07:01,660
learn it: for there are many devising evil against me."

99
00:07:01,660 --> 00:07:04,480
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

100
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:09,200
"I know. I understand.
You order a man who is thinking it too.

101
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:12,280
But come, tell me this, and tell it to me truly:

102
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:14,340
shall I go the same road with the news to Laertes

103
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:19,760
as well? Poor man — for a long
while, for all his great grief

104
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:24,260
for Odysseus, he still would oversee
the field-works, and in the house

105
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:28,560
would drink and eat with the slaves,
whenever the heart within him bade.

106
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:35,420
But now, from the day you went by ship to Pylos,
they say he no longer eats and drinks as before,

107
00:07:35,420 --> 00:07:38,800
nor looks to the field-works,
but with moaning and groaning

108
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:43,200
sits mourning; and the flesh
wastes away around his bones."

109
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:46,060
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

110
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:51,240
"The harder for it; but all the same we
will let him be, though it grieves us.

111
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:55,100
For if everything whatever could
be had for the choosing by mortals,

112
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:58,540
we would choose first the day of my father's homecoming.

113
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:04,640
No — give your message and come back; do not go
wandering over the fields after him. But tell my mother

114
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:11,140
to send her handmaid, the housekeeper, quickly —
and secretly: she could carry the news to the old man."

115
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:15,600
He spoke, and roused the swineherd,
who took his sandals in his hands,

116
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:19,580
bound them under his feet, and set off for the city.

117
00:08:19,580 --> 00:08:23,740
Escape Athena, that Eumaeus the
swineherd was gone from the farm:

118
00:08:23,740 --> 00:08:30,620
she came near; and in body she was like a woman
beautiful and tall, and skilled in glorious handwork.

119
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:34,460
She stood at the door of the hut,
showing herself to Odysseus;

120
00:08:34,460 --> 00:08:38,160
but Telemachus did not see her
before him, did not sense her —

121
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:44,620
for the gods do not appear manifest to everyone —
but Odysseus saw her, and the dogs: and they did not bark,

122
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:47,900
but whimpered and shrank in fear
to the far side of the farmstead.

123
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:52,380
She signaled with her brows;
and noble Odysseus understood,

124
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:58,640
and came out of the hall, past the great wall of the yard,
and stood before her; and Athena said to him:

125
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:02,820
"Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices:

126
00:09:02,820 --> 00:09:08,580
now speak your word to your son. Hide it no longer —
so that you two may fit death and doom to the suitors

127
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:14,300
and go to the famous city. And I myself will not
be long away from you: I am hungry for battle."

128
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:17,080
She spoke, and Athena touched him with her golden wand.

129
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:23,440
First she laid a well-washed mantle and a tunic about
his chest, and filled out his frame and his youth:

130
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:29,660
dark color came back to his skin, his jaws grew full,
and the beard about his chin turned blue-black again.

131
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:35,980
That done, she went away again; and Odysseus went
into the hut. His dear son was struck with wonder,

132
00:09:35,980 --> 00:09:42,660
and turned his eyes aside, afraid, in case it was a god,
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

133
00:09:42,660 --> 00:09:45,440
"Stranger, you look other to me now than a moment ago:

134
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:49,220
you wear other clothes; your skin is not the same.

135
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:52,760
Surely you are some god,
one of those who hold wide heaven.

136
00:09:52,760 --> 00:09:59,300
Be gracious, then! — and we will give you pleasing
sacrifices, and wrought gifts of gold. Only spare us."

137
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:02,040
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

138
00:10:02,040 --> 00:10:05,500
"I am no god. Why do you liken me to the immortals?

139
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:08,220
No: I am your father — for whose sake you go groaning

140
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:12,120
and suffer many pains,
and take men's violence on yourself."

141
00:10:12,120 --> 00:10:15,400
So speaking, he kissed his son, and down his cheeks

142
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:19,760
a tear fell to the ground. Before,
he had held them back, always.

143
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,800
But Telemachus — for he did not
yet believe it was his father —

144
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:25,740
found words again, and said to him in answer:

145
00:10:25,740 --> 00:10:32,600
"You are not Odysseus, not my father. A god is casting
a spell on me, so I may sob and grieve still more.

146
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:37,920
No mortal man could ever contrive these things
by his own wit — not unless a god in person came

147
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:41,680
and made him young or old at a stroke, at will.

148
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:47,680
Just now, I know, you were old, and meanly dressed;
and now you are like the gods who hold wide heaven."

149
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:50,820
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

150
00:10:50,820 --> 00:10:57,760
"Telemachus, it is not right, when your own father is here,
to marvel at him past measure, or stand amazed.

151
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:00,180
No other Odysseus will ever come to you here:

152
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:04,700
he is I — such as you see,
having suffered, having wandered long —

153
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:07,000
come in the twentieth year to my father's land.

154
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,460
But this — this is the work of
Athena, driver of the spoil,

155
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:16,400
who makes of me whatever she wills — for she has
the power — now like a beggar man, and now again

156
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:19,380
like a young man with fine clothes upon his body.

157
00:11:19,380 --> 00:11:25,460
It is easy for the gods who hold wide heaven
to glorify a mortal man — or to break him."

158
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:32,060
So speaking he sat down; and Telemachus, flinging his
arms around his noble father, wept and shed tears,

159
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:34,800
and in both of them rose the longing for lament.

160
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:38,460
They cried shrill, in bursts
thicker than the cries of birds —

161
00:11:38,460 --> 00:11:41,980
of ospreys, or of vultures with
crooked talons, whose young

162
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:45,460
the country people have taken from
the nest before they were fledged:

163
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:48,720
so pitifully the tears fell from beneath their brows.

164
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:52,000
And the light of the sun would
have gone down on their weeping

165
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,600
had not Telemachus suddenly spoken to his father:

166
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:58,280
"What ship was it, dear father,
whose sailors brought you here to Ithaca?

167
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:00,760
For I hardly think you came here on foot."

168
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:03,940
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus spoke to him:

169
00:12:03,940 --> 00:12:06,900
"Then I will tell you, child, the truth of it.

170
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:13,520
The Phaeacians brought me, famed for their ships,
who give passage to other men too, whoever comes to them.

171
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:20,180
They carried me asleep in a fast ship over the sea
and set me down in Ithaca, and gave me shining gifts,

172
00:12:20,180 --> 00:12:25,960
bronze and gold in plenty, and woven clothing —
all lying now in caves, by the gods' will.

173
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:31,120
And I have come here at Athena's prompting,
so we may plan together the slaughter of our enemies.

174
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:36,940
Come, count me off the suitors, one by one,
so I may know how many they are, and what men;

175
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:42,200
then I will weigh it in my blameless heart, and decide
whether we two can stand against them by ourselves,

176
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,860
alone, or should look for others."

177
00:12:44,860 --> 00:12:47,180
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

178
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:49,620
"Father, I have always heard your great fame:

179
00:12:49,620 --> 00:12:52,560
a spearman with your hands, and weighty in counsel.

180
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:56,680
But what you say is too great.
Awe holds me. It could not be

181
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:00,080
for two men to fight with many, and strong men too.

182
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:06,200
The suitors are no exact ten, nor two tens only,
but many more. You shall have the count here and now.

183
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:11,280
From Dulichium, two-and-fifty young men,
chosen, and six serving-men follow with them;

184
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:15,680
from Same there are four-and-twenty men;
from Zacynthus, twenty young Achaeans;

185
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:19,140
and from Ithaca itself, twelve, all of them the best;

186
00:13:19,140 --> 00:13:22,340
and with them are Medon the herald,
and the godlike singer,

187
00:13:22,340 --> 00:13:25,000
and two henchmen skilled in the carving of meats.

188
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,320
If we come against all of these inside the house,

189
00:13:27,320 --> 00:13:32,220
beware: your avenging of their violence
could cost too bitter, too grim a price.

190
00:13:32,220 --> 00:13:38,100
No — you, if you can think of some defender, think of
one who would stand by us two with a willing heart."

191
00:13:38,100 --> 00:13:41,300
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

192
00:13:41,300 --> 00:13:44,380
"Then I will tell you — mark me now, and listen.

193
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:47,120
Consider: will Athena, with father Zeus beside her,

194
00:13:47,120 --> 00:13:50,060
be enough for us two? Or shall
I think of some other defender?"

195
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:53,800
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

196
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:58,560
"Good defenders indeed, those two you name,
though they sit high among the clouds; and they rule

197
00:13:58,560 --> 00:14:02,240
all others — men in their power, and the deathless gods."

198
00:14:02,240 --> 00:14:05,060
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus answered him:

199
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:10,460
"Those two will not stand long apart from the strong
din of battle, when between the suitors and ourselves

200
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:13,340
the war-strength of Ares is put to trial in my halls.

201
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:18,540
But you, for now: go home at the showing of
the dawn and mix among the overweening suitors;

202
00:14:18,540 --> 00:14:23,660
and later the swineherd will bring me to the city,
looking like a beggar, wretched and old.

203
00:14:23,660 --> 00:14:28,740
And if they treat me shamefully in the house,
let the heart in your breast endure it while I suffer —

204
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:31,560
even if they drag me by the feet
through the hall to the door,

205
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:35,280
or throw things and hit me: look on, and hold yourself in.

206
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:40,100
Tell them, yes, to stop their follies —
win at them with gentle words. They will not listen,

207
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:42,980
not at all: for their fated day is standing near.

208
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:44,360
Lay it up in your heart.

209
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:49,640
When Athena of the many counsels puts it in my mind,
I will nod to you with my head; and when you see it,

210
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:54,480
take all the weapons of war that lie about the hall
and store them up in the corner of the high chamber —

211
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:59,580
all of them; and when the suitors miss them
and ask you, talk them around with soft words:

212
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:02,540
'I laid them out of the smoke:
they no longer looked like the arms

213
00:15:02,540 --> 00:15:08,480
Odysseus left behind when he went to Troy — they are
fouled over, as far as the breath of the fire reached.

214
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:11,540
And a greater fear the son of Cronos put into my heart:

215
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:15,920
that in your wine you might raise a quarrel among
you and wound one another, and shame the feast

216
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:19,640
and the wooing. For iron of itself draws a man on.'

217
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:25,060
But for us two alone leave out two swords, two spears,
and two oxhide shields for our hands to seize,

218
00:15:25,060 --> 00:15:32,700
so we can rush and take them up; and the suitors —
Pallas Athena and Zeus the counselor will daze them.

219
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:36,760
And another thing I will tell you:
lay it up in your heart.

220
00:15:36,760 --> 00:15:43,540
If you are truly mine, and truly of our blood,
then let no one hear that Odysseus is within —

221
00:15:43,540 --> 00:15:52,480
not Laertes: he is not to know it; nor the swineherd,
nor any of the household; no — not Penelope herself.

222
00:15:52,480 --> 00:15:58,360
You and I alone will learn the set of the women;
and we might test this or that man of the slaves as well:

223
00:15:58,360 --> 00:16:02,220
where one honors the two of us, and fears us in his heart,

224
00:16:02,220 --> 00:16:06,320
and where one cares nothing,
and slights you — such as you are."

225
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:08,960
Then his shining son spoke out, answered him:

226
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:14,860
"Father — you will come to know my heart hereafter,
I think; there is no slackness in me — none.

227
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:20,220
But I do not believe this plan will be a
profit to us both. Think again, I urge you.

228
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:22,720
You will be a long time going round the farms and testing

229
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:27,100
each man, one by one;
and meanwhile in the halls, at their ease,

230
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:30,680
they eat through our goods with
a high hand, and spare nothing.

231
00:16:30,680 --> 00:16:35,640
The women — yes, I would have you find out which
ones dishonor you, and which are guiltless.

232
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:41,280
But the men about the farmsteads — I for one would
rather we not test them now. Keep that work for later,

233
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:46,000
if you truly know some sign
from Zeus who bears the aegis."

234
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,480
So they spoke to each other of such things.

235
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:51,640
And meanwhile the well-built
ship was putting in to Ithaca,

236
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:55,820
the ship that carried Telemachus
from Pylos with all his companions.

237
00:16:55,820 --> 00:17:00,540
When they had come inside the deep harbor,
they hauled the black ship up onto the land,

238
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:06,400
and high-spirited henchmen carried off their gear and
took the beautiful gifts straight to Clytius' house.

239
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:12,480
And they sent a herald ahead to the house of Odysseus
to bring the news to watchful-minded Penelope —

240
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:18,360
that Telemachus was in the country, and had ordered
the ship to sail on to the city — lest the strong queen

241
00:17:18,360 --> 00:17:21,920
take fright at heart, and let the soft tear fall.

242
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:25,200
And the two of them met,
the herald and the noble swineherd,

243
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:28,100
come on the same errand, to bring the woman word.

244
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:33,500
But when they reached the house of the godlike king,
the herald spoke out in the midst of the slave-women:

245
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:36,500
"Already, O queen, your dear son has come home."

246
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:42,620
But the swineherd stood close to Penelope and told
her everything her dear son had charged him to say.

247
00:17:42,620 --> 00:17:44,960
And when he had delivered the whole of his commission,

248
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:48,600
he went his way to his swine,
and left the walls and the hall.

249
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:54,840
But the suitors were downcast, dejected in spirit,
and came out of the hall past the great wall of the yard,

250
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:57,840
and there before the gates they sat themselves down.

251
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:02,180
And Eurymachus, Polybus' son, began to speak among them:

252
00:18:02,180 --> 00:18:05,220
"Friends, here is a great work brought off insolently —

253
00:18:05,220 --> 00:18:09,200
this voyage of Telemachus.
We swore it would never be done.

254
00:18:09,200 --> 00:18:15,060
Come — let us drag down a black ship, the best we have,
and gather seamen for rowers, to carry word

255
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:18,520
quickly to those two out there: come home at speed."

256
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:23,960
He had not finished speaking when Amphinomus,
turning in his place, saw the ship inside the deep harbor,

257
00:18:23,960 --> 00:18:27,360
the crew furling the sail and
holding the oars in their hands.

258
00:18:27,360 --> 00:18:31,160
He broke into easy laughter and called to his companions:

259
00:18:31,160 --> 00:18:34,260
"No need now to send any message. They are in.

260
00:18:34,260 --> 00:18:39,640
Either some god told them, or they saw the ship
themselves passing by — and could not catch her."

261
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,380
So he spoke; and they rose and
went to the shore of the sea.

262
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:48,740
Quickly the men hauled the black ship up onto the land,
and high-spirited henchmen carried off their gear;

263
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:52,100
and they themselves went in a body
to the assembly-place, and allowed

264
00:18:52,100 --> 00:18:55,580
no one else to sit among them, young or old.

265
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:58,780
And Antinous spoke among them, Eupeithes' son:

266
00:18:58,780 --> 00:19:02,860
"Damn it all — see how the gods have
slipped this man out of harm's reach!

267
00:19:02,860 --> 00:19:06,000
Day after day our watchers sat on the windy headlands,

268
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,680
relief upon relief of them; and with the sun gone down
we never passed a night ashore, but afloat on the sea

269
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:17,220
in our fast ship we waited for the bright Dawn,
lying in ambush for Telemachus, to take him

270
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:21,320
and kill him. And all the while
some god has brought him home.

271
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:23,140
Then let us here devise a wretched death

272
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:27,460
for Telemachus — not let him slip
between our hands. For I tell you,

273
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:31,200
while he lives, this business
of ours will not be brought off.

274
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:36,080
In counsel and in mind, and the people
no longer look on us with any favor.

275
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:42,840
Act, then — before he can call the Achaeans together
to assembly. He will not hang back, I promise you:

276
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:46,280
he will keep his anger; he will
stand up among them all and tell

277
00:19:46,280 --> 00:19:49,820
how we wove sheer murder for him —
and could not catch him.

278
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:52,860
And they will not praise us, hearing of evil deeds.

279
00:19:52,860 --> 00:19:59,160
Beware they do not do us harm, and drive us out of
our own country, and we come to the land of strangers.

280
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:02,300
Take him first: catch him in the field, far from the city,

281
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:06,280
or on the road. His livelihood
and goods we hold ourselves,

282
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:09,060
dividing them share and share among us; the house

283
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:13,240
we would give his mother to keep —
hers and the man who weds her.

284
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:20,620
But if this word displeases you, and you choose instead
that he live, and keep all that was his father's —

285
00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:23,640
then let us not go on gathering here to eat away

286
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:27,780
his store of pleasant things.
Let each man from his own hall

287
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:31,280
court her with bride-gifts, seeking her; and she then

288
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:35,100
may wed the man who gives the most,
and comes as her fate."

289
00:20:35,100 --> 00:20:38,580
So he spoke; and they all fell silent and still.

290
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:45,840
Then Amphinomus rose and spoke among them —
the shining son of Nisus, of the lord Aretias' line,

291
00:20:45,840 --> 00:20:51,700
who from Dulichium, land of wheat and grass,
led the suitors; and beyond the others he pleased

292
00:20:51,700 --> 00:20:55,220
Penelope with his talk, for his mind was set on good.

293
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:58,220
With good intent he rose and spoke among them:

294
00:20:58,220 --> 00:21:04,580
"Friends, I for one would have no wish to kill Telemachus.
Dreadful it is to kill the blood of kings.

295
00:21:04,580 --> 00:21:08,080
No — first let us ask what is the counsel of the gods.

296
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:13,580
If the ordinances of great Zeus approve it,
I will kill him myself, and bid all the rest to help;

297
00:21:13,580 --> 00:21:17,640
but if the gods turn us aside, I say: stop here."

298
00:21:17,640 --> 00:21:21,400
So spoke Amphinomus, and his word pleased them well.

299
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:26,920
They rose at once and went to the house of Odysseus,
and coming in they sat on the polished chairs.

300
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:30,160
Then watchful-minded Penelope thought of one thing more:

301
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:33,440
to show herself to the suitors
in their overweening insolence.

302
00:21:33,440 --> 00:21:36,260
For she had learned of her son's
death plotted in the halls —

303
00:21:36,260 --> 00:21:39,820
the herald Medon told her,
who had overheard their counsels.

304
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:42,560
She went down to the hall with her attendant women.

305
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:47,700
And when she reached the suitors, shining among women,
she stood beside the pillar of the close-built roof,

306
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:54,440
holding her glistening veil before her cheeks,
and rebuked Antinous, and spoke a word, and named him:

307
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:58,160
"Antinous — insolence itself,
deviser of evils! And they say

308
00:21:58,160 --> 00:22:00,880
that in the land of Ithaca you are the best of your years

309
00:22:00,880 --> 00:22:04,560
in counsel and in speech. You were never such a man.

310
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:09,680
Madman! Why do you weave death and doom for Telemachus,
and pay no heed to suppliants — over whom

311
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:14,120
Zeus stands witness? It is unholy
to weave evil against each other.

312
00:22:14,120 --> 00:22:16,740
Do you not know the day your father came here in flight,

313
00:22:16,740 --> 00:22:21,700
in terror of the people? For they were bitterly enraged,
because he had joined with Taphian pirates

314
00:22:21,700 --> 00:22:24,980
to harry the Thesprotians — men who were our allies.

315
00:22:24,980 --> 00:22:28,480
They wanted to destroy him,
to tear the dear heart out of him,

316
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:31,700
and to swallow his great and pleasing livelihood whole;

317
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:34,860
but Odysseus held them back and
checked them, hot as they were.

318
00:22:34,860 --> 00:22:40,920
His house you now eat up scot-free; you woo his wife;
you would kill his son; and me you grieve to the core.

319
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:44,740
Stop, I command you. And command the rest to stop."

320
00:22:44,740 --> 00:22:47,620
Then Eurymachus, Polybus' son, answered her:

321
00:22:47,620 --> 00:22:53,800
"Daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope,
take heart. Do not let these things trouble your mind.

322
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:59,240
The man does not exist, will not exist, cannot be
born who shall lay hands upon your son Telemachus

323
00:22:59,240 --> 00:23:02,480
while I am alive and look upon the light of the earth.

324
00:23:02,480 --> 00:23:06,100
For I will say this outright, and it will be accomplished:

325
00:23:06,100 --> 00:23:09,240
in an instant his black blood would run down my spear —

326
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:15,220
that man's. For Odysseus, sacker of cities —
many a time he sat me on his knees, and put roast meat

327
00:23:15,220 --> 00:23:18,480
into my hands, and held the red wine to my lips.

328
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:24,180
And so Telemachus is to me by far the dearest of all men;
and I tell him he need not tremble at death —

329
00:23:24,180 --> 00:23:27,740
not from the suitors. From the gods there is no escaping."

330
00:23:27,740 --> 00:23:32,240
So he spoke to soothe her — and was
himself preparing the boy's destruction.

331
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:37,380
But she climbed to her shining upper room,
and wept there for Odysseus, her dear husband, until

332
00:23:37,380 --> 00:23:40,740
gray-eyed Athena cast sweet sleep upon her eyelids.

333
00:23:40,740 --> 00:23:46,240
And at evening the noble swineherd came back to
Odysseus and his son. They were making their supper,

334
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:53,780
busy about it, having killed a year-old pig.
But Athena came and stood close by Laertes' son Odysseus,

335
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:56,880
and struck him with her wand
and made him an old man again,

336
00:23:56,880 --> 00:24:00,820
and dressed his body in miserable
clothes — lest the swineherd

337
00:24:00,820 --> 00:24:04,220
look at him face to face and
know him, and go with the news

338
00:24:04,220 --> 00:24:08,340
to steady-hearted Penelope,
not holding it fast in his heart.

339
00:24:08,340 --> 00:24:11,020
And Telemachus was first to speak to him:

340
00:24:11,020 --> 00:24:14,500
"You have come, noble Eumaeus. What news in the town?

341
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:20,460
Are the haughty suitors back inside by now, home from
their ambush — or still watching for me on my way?"

342
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:22,820
And you answered him, Eumaeus the swineherd:

343
00:24:22,820 --> 00:24:28,500
"I had no mind to ask about that, going down
through the town. My heart said: tell your message

344
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:31,060
and be quick, and come back here again.

345
00:24:31,060 --> 00:24:36,780
And a swift messenger from your companions overtook me,
a herald — and he spoke to your mother first.

346
00:24:36,780 --> 00:24:40,340
But this much more I know; this I saw with my eyes.

347
00:24:40,340 --> 00:24:46,320
I was already above the city, where the hill of Hermes
stands, on my way, when I saw a fast ship coming down

348
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:52,460
into our harbor. There were many men aboard her,
and she rode heavy with shields and double-pointed spears.

349
00:24:52,460 --> 00:24:55,420
I thought it was they — but I cannot know."

350
00:24:55,420 --> 00:24:58,760
So he spoke; and the sacred strength of Telemachus smiled,

351
00:24:58,760 --> 00:25:02,640
and his eyes went to his father —
slipping past the swineherd's.

352
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:05,400
And when they had ceased their
labor and made the meal ready,

353
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:09,520
they feasted; and no man's appetite
went short of the equal feast.

354
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:12,460
But when they had put away desire for food and drink,

355
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:15,456
they remembered their beds, and took the gift of sleep.
